  "content": {
    "id": "p25-q1-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "1",
    "prompt": "Write the composite function $f(g(x))$ and state its domain and range.",
    "layout": "grid",
    "columns": 2,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p25-q1-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "$f(x)=x^2$ and $g(x)=x-1$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$f(g(x))=x^2-2x+1$; domain: $(-\\infty,\\infty)$; range: $[0,\\infty)$.",
          "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}f(g(x))&=(x-1)^2\\\\f(g(x))&=x^2-2x+1\\end{align*}$$\nThe expression is defined for every real $x$, so the domain is $(-\\infty,\\infty)$. Since $(x-1)^2\\geq0$, with minimum $0$ at $x=1$ and no upper bound, the range is $[0,\\infty)$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 22
      },
      {
        "id": "p25-q1-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "$f(x)=x^3$ and $g(x)=x+5$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$f(g(x))=(x+5)^3$; domain: $(-\\infty,\\infty)$; range: $(-\\infty,\\infty)$.",
          "worked": "$$f(g(x))=(x+5)^3$$\nThe expression is defined for every real $x$, so the domain is $(-\\infty,\\infty)$. As $x+5$ takes every real value, its cube also takes every real value. The range is $(-\\infty,\\infty)$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 22
      },
      {
        "id": "p25-q1-c",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "c",
        "prompt": "$f(x)=\\sqrt{x}$ and $g(x)=x-2$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$f(g(x))=\\sqrt{x-2}$; domain: $[2,\\infty)$; range: $[0,\\infty)$.",
          "worked": "$$f(g(x))=\\sqrt{x-2}$$\nThe radicand must be non-negative:\n$$\\begin{align*}x-2&\\geq0\\\\x&\\geq2\\end{align*}$$\nThe domain is $[2,\\infty)$. The square root has minimum $0$ at $x=2$ and no upper bound, so the range is $[0,\\infty)$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 25
      },
      {
        "id": "p25-q1-d",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "d",
        "prompt": "$f(x)=-\\sqrt{x}$ and $g(x)=3x+9$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$f(g(x))=-\\sqrt{3x+9}$; domain: $[-3,\\infty)$; range: $(-\\infty,0]$.",
          "worked": "$$f(g(x))=-\\sqrt{3x+9}$$\nThe radicand must be non-negative:\n$$\\begin{align*}3x+9&\\geq0\\\\3x&\\geq-9\\\\x&\\geq-3\\end{align*}$$\nThe domain is $[-3,\\infty)$. The negative square root has maximum $0$ at $x=-3$ and no lower bound, so the range is $(-\\infty,0]$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 25
      },
      {
        "id": "p25-q1-e",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "e",
        "prompt": "$f(x)=\\sqrt{x}$ and $g(x)=4-x^2$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$f(g(x))=\\sqrt{4-x^2}$; domain: $[-2,2]$; range: $[0,2]$.",
          "worked": "$$f(g(x))=\\sqrt{4-x^2}$$\nThe radicand must be non-negative:\n$$\\begin{align*}4-x^2&\\geq0\\\\x^2&\\leq4\\\\-2\\leq x&\\leq2\\end{align*}$$\nThe domain is $[-2,2]$. On this domain, $4-x^2$ takes every value from $0$ to $4$, so $\\sqrt{4-x^2}$ takes every value from $0$ to $2$. The range is $[0,2]$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 30
      },
      {
        "id": "p25-q1-f",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "f",
        "prompt": "$f(x)=-\\sqrt{x}$ and $g(x)=1-x^2$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$f(g(x))=-\\sqrt{1-x^2}$; domain: $[-1,1]$; range: $[-1,0]$.",
          "worked": "$$f(g(x))=-\\sqrt{1-x^2}$$\nThe radicand must be non-negative:\n$$\\begin{align*}1-x^2&\\geq0\\\\x^2&\\leq1\\\\-1\\leq x&\\leq1\\end{align*}$$\nThe domain is $[-1,1]$. On this domain, $\\sqrt{1-x^2}$ takes every value from $0$ to $1$. Negating gives the range $[-1,0]$, with minimum $-1$ at $x=0$ and maximum $0$ at $x=\\pm1$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 30
      }
    ]
